        {
          "type": "heading",
          "text": "5. Rotated Sorted Array"
        },
        {
          "type": "sidebox",
          "title": "1. What is a Rotated Sorted Array?",
          "items": [
            "Sorted ascending, then rotated at an unknown ==pivot==",
            "Original: [1,2,3,4,5,6,7]",
            "Rotated: [4,5,6,7,**1**,2,3]",
            "Pivot = element 1 (the minimum)"
          ],
          "ink": "green"
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "lang": "java",
          "lines": [
            "int low = 0, high = n - 1;",
            "while (low < high) {",
            "  mid = low + (high - low) / 2;",
            "  if (arr[mid] > arr[high])",
            "    low = mid + 1;",
            "  else high = mid; // pivot left",
            "}",
            "return low; // index of minimum"
          ],
          "note": "compare mid with high, not low"
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "tip",
          "title": "Why arr[mid] > arr[high]?",
          "text": "If mid is greater than the last element, the minimum must be in the **RIGHT** half. Otherwise it is in the LEFT half, including mid."
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "style": "sticky",
          "title": "Key Idea",
          "text": "At least ==one half is always sorted==. Check which half is sorted, then decide whether the target lies inside it. Dry run: arr = [4,5,6,7,1,2,3], target = 2."
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
